154 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (        Random Forest was the strongest model among the three approaches tested. Its nonlinear structure is well suited to relationships between temperature and meteorological variables that may not be adequately represented by a linear model. The tuned Random Forest is arguably the more defensible final model for presentation because it provides similar test performance while reducing the extreme training performance of the original model. The difference between the tuned model's cross-validation score and held-out test score is also relatively small, supporting reasonably stable generalization within this dataset. However, the model should be interpreted as a baseline predictive study rather than a complete operational forecasting system. Its performance is specific to the dataset, location, feature set, and evaluation design used in this project.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 59 0 R
/T (10. Limitations)
/E (10. Limitations)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 227 0 R
/E (Single location: The dataset represents one geographic location in Giza. The model should not be assumed to generalize to other parts of Egypt without additional spatial data and validation.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 228 0 R
/E (No lagged temperature features: The model does not explicitly use previous temperature observations such as 1-hour, 24-hour, or 7-day lags. These could be important predictors because temperature is temporally autocorrelated.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 229 0 R
/E (Forecasting horizon: The current experiment evaluates predictions on a later historical period but does not define a specific operational forecast horizon such as 1 hour ahead or 24 hours ahead.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
